nimony learns a **Temen backend** (`nimony t`), so the self-hosted lane's driver plans the whole build.

Why: the self-hosted lane (#1609) runs nimony's own driver in-guest on Temen. With the C backend the
plan ends in lengc + cc + a linker, none of which exist there, so the host used to run hexer and the
link itself. With this backend the driver's plan is the whole build and the host only seeds sources.

What: the C backend's pipeline unchanged up to the DCE'd `.c.nif` (same target, same hexer, same
flags, so every artifact up to there is byte-identical to `nimony c`'s), then ONE whole-program
node in place of lengc + cc + link: `temen-link -o:<prog.temen> <every module's .c.nif, main
first>`, which writes one verified Temen module. It sits beside the web backends (`backendWasm` /
`backendJs`, whose whole-program tool is jorogumo): the same shape, an output-naming helper beside
theirs (`temenFile`), and its own backend directory (`<main>.temen/`). A Temen module runs under a Temen host, so `nimony t -r`
runs it only where nimony itself runs on one: it execs it there, as a native nimony execs its
executable, and refuses anywhere else. `temen-link` itself lives in this repo
(`crates/temen-run/demos/temen_link`).

A program built for Temen sees `defined(temen)`: its target CPU/OS are the C backend's, but it runs
under a Temen host. The define changes no artifact of code that does not test it (the `.p/.s/.x/.c.nif`
of a program built with and without it are byte-identical). The compiler relies on it: built for
Temen, it builds the programs it runs itself for Temen too, since there is no C compiler under a
Temen host and only Temen modules run there. So `prepareEval` precompiles `std/writenif` with `t`, a
plugin is built with `t`, and `nimony s` (the program a compile-time evaluation runs) uses the Temen
backend, whose output `runProgram` then executes. A macro's plugin is built the same way (#2046): its
setup build with `t`, and nimony found with `findTool`, as `semos` finds it, since a Temen host
serves no `/proc/self/exe` and `getAppDir()` is empty there. Where the plugin's platform is the outer
build's (no target triple, as on Temen), it builds as compile-time evaluation builds its program, in
the outer cache with the outer command line, instead of compiling the library again in a cache of
its own (#2049); its executable is `<stem>.plugin`, since `<stem>` (`<stem>.temen` for `t`) is the
plugin module's backend directory there. `nimsem e` (`buildGraphForEval`) still plans the C
toolchain itself; no build uses it.

Applied to a COPY of the pinned sources at build time (`scripts/nim-toolchain.sh`); the
vendored `nimony/` submodule is never modified. Against the submodule pin (6ca46f17).
Drop it when upstream nimony has a Temen backend — it is written to be upstreamed as is.
